{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QvKDT6EvEdWe6BDC951yvL","type":"tool_result","content":"257:[Omitted long matching line]\n522:That is the whole of the core idea: a namespace is a **scope for names**. Two teams can both have a Service called `database` and neither has to rename anything, because the two names never collide. They sit in different scopes. Two ships on two different registries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\n554: ║   │    Service    \"database\"   │  │    Service    \"database\"   │ ║\n588:[Omitted long matching line]\n600:[Omitted long matching line]\n732:A) Deployment, Service, Pod\n835:[Omitted long matching line]\n837:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Once you internalize that all of those are the same mechanism pointed at different things, four chapters get considerably easier. ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are not four mechanisms to learn. They are one mechanism, *describe a set by its attributes*, aimed at four problems. Learn the primitive once and you spend the later chapters learning what each resource *does* with its set, which is the interesting part.\n877:[Omitted long matching line]\n1262:- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6100e90-d251-4659-a972-faa45e77104d","uuid":"499893c0-07e2-4fc9-9636-7811eb5a1894","timestamp":"2026-08-25T01:59:08.806Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"257:[Omitted long matching line]\n522:That is the whole of the core idea: a namespace is a **scope for names**. Two teams can both have a Service called `database` and neither has to rename anything, because the two names never collide. They sit in different scopes. Two ships on two different registries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\n554: ║   │    Service    \"database\"   │  │    Service    \"database\"   │ ║\n588:[Omitted long matching line]\n600:[Omitted long matching line]\n732:A) Deployment, Service, Pod\n835:[Omitted long matching line]\n837:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Once you internalize that all of those are the same mechanism pointed at different things, four chapters get considerably easier. ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are not four mechanisms to learn. They are one mechanism, *describe a set by its attributes*, aimed at four problems. Learn the primitive once and you spend the later chapters learning what each resource *does* with its set, which is the interesting part.\n877:[Omitted long matching line]\n1262:- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not.","numLines":10,"totalLines":10}}
