{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wae9eBzhX4FTkuCkR1xvxU","type":"tool_result","content":"266:8. **`ImagePullBackOff`** — and Chapter 2 said it is reported as a container in the **Waiting** state. If you missed this, re-read Chapter 2 §6 before §5.\n620:[Omitted long matching line]\n656:Chapter 2 named a failure and deferred it here: `ImagePullBackOff` *[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §6 — ImagePullBackOff and where its state is defined]*. It's the cleanest possible demonstration of the phase/state split.\n658:[Omitted long matching line]\n666:| Container state `Reason` | `ImagePullBackOff` — *this specific* reason it can't start | Container |\n672:[Omitted long matching line]\n720:**4. Phase: `Pending`. Container state: `Waiting`. The field: `Reason`, carrying the value `ImagePullBackOff`.**\n722:The phase is `Pending` because the Pod has been accepted but its container is not yet set up and running [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. The container is `Waiting` because it is still doing what it needs in order to start, and `Waiting` carries a `Reason` field that summarizes why [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. `ImagePullBackOff` means the container could not start because Kubernetes could not pull the image [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n727:- **\"State: `ImagePullBackOff`.\"** `ImagePullBackOff` is a **`Reason`**, not a state. The three container states are `Waiting`, `Running`, and `Terminated`, and nothing else is ever one of them. If you wrote the right string in the wrong slot, you have the fact and not yet the taxonomy, and that is precisely the error §5's hazards block exists to close. It is also the most self-concealing miss in the chapter, because the correct string appears in your answer and it *looks* right.\n969:[Omitted long matching line]\n1027:This item required §5 and §8 together, which is the point: it's the direct precursor to Chapter 13's material on diagnosing killed and evicted Pods. What we're **not** doing here is diagnosing it. Naming the mechanism is this chapter's job. Which command to run and which events to read is Chapter 13's *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §4 — OOMKilled and Evicted]*.\n1414:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6100e90-d251-4659-a972-faa45e77104d","uuid":"d26338e9-69d4-4d15-afd3-2f9edec512d2","timestamp":"2026-08-25T02:02:59.940Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"266:8. **`ImagePullBackOff`** — and Chapter 2 said it is reported as a container in the **Waiting** state. If you missed this, re-read Chapter 2 §6 before §5.\n620:[Omitted long matching line]\n656:Chapter 2 named a failure and deferred it here: `ImagePullBackOff` *[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §6 — ImagePullBackOff and where its state is defined]*. It's the cleanest possible demonstration of the phase/state split.\n658:[Omitted long matching line]\n666:| Container state `Reason` | `ImagePullBackOff` — *this specific* reason it can't start | Container |\n672:[Omitted long matching line]\n720:**4. Phase: `Pending`. Container state: `Waiting`. The field: `Reason`, carrying the value `ImagePullBackOff`.**\n722:The phase is `Pending` because the Pod has been accepted but its container is not yet set up and running [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. The container is `Waiting` because it is still doing what it needs in order to start, and `Waiting` carries a `Reason` field that summarizes why [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. `ImagePullBackOff` means the container could not start because Kubernetes could not pull the image [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n727:- **\"State: `ImagePullBackOff`.\"** `ImagePullBackOff` is a **`Reason`**, not a state. The three container states are `Waiting`, `Running`, and `Terminated`, and nothing else is ever one of them. If you wrote the right string in the wrong slot, you have the fact and not yet the taxonomy, and that is precisely the error §5's hazards block exists to close. It is also the most self-concealing miss in the chapter, because the correct string appears in your answer and it *looks* right.\n969:[Omitted long matching line]\n1027:This item required §5 and §8 together, which is the point: it's the direct precursor to Chapter 13's material on diagnosing killed and evicted Pods. What we're **not** doing here is diagnosing it. Naming the mechanism is this chapter's job. Which command to run and which events to read is Chapter 13's *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §4 — OOMKilled and Evicted]*.\n1414:[Omitted long matching line]","numLines":12,"totalLines":12}}
